WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.320
What should a campaign ask before
releasing a new message?

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:07.800
Imagine writing directions for someone who
has never seen your map.

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:11.980
The words may make sense to your team and
still leave the reader lost.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:17.580
Campaign message testing starts by writing
down what the team means.

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:21.960
Then ask people what they hear, in their
own words, before explaining the

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:25.500
intent. Listen for missing facts,
alternative meanings

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:27.400
and claims they want supported.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:30.960
A phrase like “more access” may prompt a
simple question:

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:32.380
access to what,

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:34.000
for whom and when?

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:38.160
A useful revision answers the question
rather than adding a louder

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:38.880
adjective.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:41.880
Qualitative message testing reveals
interpretation.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:46.460
It does not prove a message will persuade
voters or predict an election.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:51.800
PRC Impact helps teams examine draft
language before broader use.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:56.900
Discuss your message question at
prcimpact.com, or call

00:00:56.900 --> 00:01:01.080
334-429-6994.
